## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to diagnose production latency where security tooling (TLS inspection) interacts with edge caches and rate limits.

## Key Concepts

- TLS inspection overhead and queueing
- Firewall connection limits and burst behavior
- Edge CDN caching and TTLs
- Observability: edge logs, pcap, and curl timing data

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple latency probe using fetch
async function probe(url){
  const t0 = performance.now();
  await fetch(url);
  const t1 = performance.now();
  console.log('latency', t1 - t0);
}
probe('https://edge.example/login');
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you verify bypass granularity while maintaining security?
- What metrics would you monitor during a peak event to alert on this issue?

## Why This Is Asked
This question exams diagnosing subtle cross-region telemetry issues tied to time synchronization, control-plane stability, MTU handling, and NIC behavior, not just data-path faults.

## Key Concepts
- Time synchronization protocols (NTP/PTP)
- EVPN route convergence and BFD timers
- UDP telemetry MTU/fragmentation implications
- NIC offloads and timestamp behavior

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: quick log parser for interval spikes
const fs=require('fs');
const lines=fs.readFileSync(process.argv[2],'utf8').split('\n');
let spikes=0;
lines.forEach(l=>{ if(/telemetry.*tail/.test(l)) spikes++;});
console.log('spikes',spikes);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you automate this diagnostic with a runbook?
- What thresholds would trigger configuration changes (timers, MTU, pacing)?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical DNS- and LB-related troubleshooting that intermediate engineers confront in global deployments. It emphasizes data collection, reproducibility, and concrete mitigations rather than theory.

## Key Concepts
- DNS TTL and resolver caching
- Geolocation-based routing rules
- Regional load balancer health checks and failover
- Traceroute/tracepath, dig +trace, and MTR for path visibility

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple DNS lookup example using Node.js
const dns = require('dns');
dns.resolve4('api.example.com', (err, addresses) => {
  console.log(addresses, err);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you design a minimal reproducible test for this issue?
- What mitigations would you apply if you must avoid application changes?
